```c
case MQTT_PROP_TYPE_BINARY_DATA:
  if (i + 2 > end) return 0;
  prop->val.len = (uint16_t) ((((uint16_t) i[0]) << 8) | i[1]);
  prop->val.buf = (char *) i + 2;
  new_pos += 2 + prop->val.len;
  break;
```

The check `if (i + 2 > end) return 0;` validates that the two-byte length field can be read, but it does NOT validate that `i + 2 + prop->val.len <= end`. So if `prop->val.len` is large, `prop->val.buf` points beyond the buffer, and later processing consumes bytes beyond the input buffer.

Compare with `MQTT_PROP_TYPE_STRING`:
```c
case MQTT_PROP_TYPE_STRING:
  if (i + 2 >= end) return 0;
  prop->val.len = (uint16_t) ((((uint16_t) i[0]) << 8) | i[1]);
  prop->val.buf = (char *) i + 2;
  if (i + 2 + prop->val.len > end) return 0;  // <-- this check exists for STRING
  new_pos += 2 + prop->val.len;
  break;
```

So the STRING case has the `if (i + 2 + prop->val.len > end) return 0;` check, but the BINARY_DATA case does NOT. That's the bug.

